pied along diagonals determined by the coordinate shifts. The key is now understanding the background value, which I've determined to be the most frequent color in the input grid. This seems to be the key to understanding the full transformation.\n\n\n**Validating Shift Parameters**\n\nI'm now refining the shift calculations based on observations from Training examples 1, 2, and 3. I am investigating the possible values of the shift, k. I realized k is not just a free parameter, but is constrained. Specifically, it seems each cell (r,c) in the input maps to a diagonal in the output, where (r+k, c+k) must be a valid output cell (within bounds). I'm testing this new logic, and focusing on edge cases. I'm checking why (r+k) must be valid!\n\n\n",
